      "textSha256": "625b0d17f81beabee65cfe8715e77895a298cceaa342a12dfe6599ef35a80874",
      "change": "Scope the material statement to ordinary atomic matter; distinguish positive nuclei and negative electrons."
    },
    {
      "index": 1,
      "sourceRefs": ["p0149#7"],
      "textSha256": "b97e6bc5288c9bc4486d502930da806c9d7179b50a15bcc4fc907ec8ac3d34b2",
      "change": "Correct the source atom-count/electron-count explanation in n60, not n59. Neutrality compares total positive nuclear charge and total negative electron charge; a neutral atom has equal proton/electron counts. Include ion and polarization qualifications."
    },
    {
      "index": 2,
      "sourceRefs": ["p0149#8"],
      "textSha256": "dee84f51a492f77fb7029dbd7724dab49a829bae7a47850bd210d9b675a9d52c",
      "change": "Net charge arises from electron gain/loss relative to neutrality in the ordinary unchanged-nucleus transfer model."
    },
    {
      "index": 3,
      "sourceRefs": ["p0150#1"],
      "textSha256": "68fb83f4d6d30dd1d024265d522831d61b1cb8ecbdf564f90bf825d00847410f",
      "change": "Retain the comb-negative/hair-positive source example conditionally; contact transfer does not create charge and signs depend on material/surface conditions."
    },
    {
      "index": 4,
      "sourceRefs": ["p0150#6"],
      "textSha256": "a1dbeee5a7cc0e67adf093f27c321857ee3bd925617b00329b29908957540197",
      "change": "Retain the bound footnote and explain charge as a carrier property rather than a free-standing transported substance."
    },
    {
      "index": 5,
      "sourceRefs": ["p0150#2", "p0150#5"],
      "textSha256": "32c09de5e880d616fcfb8dddf285e0d1cfa8fd8e2fd3d230d7a757d595e7a7ff",
      "change": "Retain the two-disc, bucket/axis/gold-leaf apparatus and all three source observations. Condition equal/opposite net charge on initially neutral isolated exchange, and electroscope interpretation on insulation, no wall contact, comparable placement and negligible edge/leakage effects. Separate induction from net-charge transfer; no arbitrary-angle exact measurement claim."
    },
    {
      "index": 8,
      "sourceRefs": ["p0151#1", "p0151#4"],
      "textSha256": "21a53e4c5fc94b525a586cbf713537f64cea0413665c49d09a1656bab7b81a22",
      "change": "Retain the exact10^-8cm expression as a local scale illustration, not a universal maximum displacement. Identify electron transfer as a simplified contact model, not every material's only mechanism; no fig105 small-ball decoding."
    },
    {
      "index": 11,
      "sourceRefs": ["p0151#5"],
      "textSha256": "a62ac9ed43e3c70f4373e1ddd91da6daf297fb2f6eacf057f925983fa270dc16",
      "change": "Qualify microscopic roughness, real contact and repeated-contact effects; transferred charge is not universally proportional to friction count or contact area."
    },
    {
      "index": 12,
      "sourceRefs": ["p0151#6"],
      "textSha256": "cfa9caefd9be1c6cccb7e68daa93c8cf23762a437b2acc95e7cb4724da2eccad",
      "change": "Remove universal contact-charging and total-electron-return claims; distinguish mobility, leakage, grounding/insulation and retained net charge."
    },
    {
      "index": 13,
      "sourceRefs": ["p0151#7"],
      "textSha256": "8a589015788eef3ec2d3245979280af15a21309ca5d250c066cefcd6f511b19a",
      "change": "Retain the insulated-handle metal-disc/cloth example conditionally without promising fixed charge sign or magnitude."
    },
    {
      "index": 14,
      "sourceRefs": ["p0151#8"],
      "textSha256": "40391e7c978ed30b89b8063f031826b98ca453467b60632555d49b6324f9547b",
      "change": "Retain nylon, belt/pulley and spark examples conditionally. Combustible medium, suitable concentration and ignition energy govern fire/explosion risk; no deliberate discharge experiment."
    },
    {
      "index": 15,
      "sourceRefs": ["p0151#9", "p0152#1"],
      "textSha256": "dfa6c46b5d66d6cf10360138df78f6d725b0b95b82172b34a138649a275d3a2f",
      "change": "Retain the source printing/paper/rubber-glove situation as historical description, not a universal modern antistatic prescription. Ordinary rubber isolation does not eliminate accumulated charge or guarantee safety."
